{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CtrCGN4i4PSH5Kf9S1BdVF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KyvCa3D6zFeJaDds97f4em","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment — R2: Novelty & Related Work\n\nYou are reviewing a paper titled **\"Differential Depth Response in Recursive LLM Generation: Classifying Generation Depth Beyond Binary Detection\"** for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n### Paper Summary\nThe paper formulates \"generation depth classification\" as a new task beyond binary AI-text detection: classify text as gen-0 (human), gen-1 (first-generation), or gen-2+ (recursive). The core concept is **Differential Depth Response (DDR)**: central-tendency features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) drift monotonically with depth while shape features (entropy max, tail decay) remain near-stationary. A 7-feature XGBoost ensemble achieves LOGO binary F1 = 0.680 across 5 base-model generators from 3 families. 15 experiments cover ablation, cross-generator/family transfer, reference model validation, prompt types, paraphrastic chains, cross-model chains, multi-domain, ordinal resolution, robustness, LD-Score, QLoRA end-to-end, length control, scale effects, and baseline comparisons. Key negative finding: depth-aware curation ≈ random subsampling.\n\n### Your Focus: Novelty & Related Work\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly:\n\n1. **Novelty of Task Formulation**\n   - Is \"generation depth classification\" genuinely new, or a trivial extension of binary detection?\n   - 5-class F1 = 0.468 with gen-2/3/4 near-uniform confusion — does the data actually support more than ~2.5 classes? If the signal is essentially binary-plus-noise, is the 3-class formulation a real contribution?\n   - How does this relate to Briesch et al. (2023) \"broken telephones\" and Guo et al. (2024) attractor convergence?\n\n2. **Novelty of DDR Concept**\n   - Is DDR a genuinely new phenomenon or repackaged vocabulary impoverishment?\n   - All drifting features (hapax, TTR, entropy_mean) decline monotonically — are these just different measurements of the same well-known effect?\n   - The drifting/stationary dichotomy: entropy_std sits at R² = 0.41 (ambiguous). Does this weaken the claimed clean bimodal split?\n   - Drift magnitude varies 2× across reference models — does this mean DDR partially reflects reference-generator mismatch rather than intrinsic recursive generation dynamics?\n\n3. **Related Work Completeness**\n   - The paper cites DetectGPT, FastDetectGPT, Binoculars, GLTR, DNA-GPT for detection; Shumailov et al., Briesch et al., Guo et al. for model collapse/iterative generation; Penedo et al., Lee et al. for data quality.\n   - Are there missing related works? Consider: watermarking methods, iterative self-refinement literature, recursive text generation in NLG, stylometric analysis of machine text, multi-class AI text detection.\n   - Is the connection to vocabulary impoverishment / model collapse literature sufficiently differentiated?\n\n4. **Contribution Assessment**\n   - Contribution 1 (task formulation + 7-feature ensemble): LOGO F1 = 0.680 — is this sufficient signal to justify a new task?\n   - Contribution 2 (DDR characterization): Is the drifting/stationary distinction a genuine insight or an artifact of feature selection?\n   - Contribution 3 (negative finding): Depth-aware curation ≈ random subsampling — is a negative finding sufficient contribution for a top venue? Does it raise \"why does this matter\" concerns?\n   - The multi-detector ensemble (0.708) surpasses 7-feature (0.680) — does this undermine the proposed method's reason to exist?\n\n5. **Framing & Positioning**\n   - The paper frames depth fingerprinting as \"diagnostic not curative\" — is a diagnostic tool with 0.680 F1 useful?\n   - The paper acknowledges scope limitations honestly (base models only, temperature-sensitive, edit-fragile, scale-dependent) — but does this honesty actually strengthen or weaken the paper?\n   - Is this better positioned as \"empirical study of recursive generation dynamics\" rather than \"depth classification as a tool\"?\n   - 33 pages of content across the paper — is this focused enough or an unfocused experiment dump?\n\n6. **Writing Quality & Presentation**\n   - Is the paper clearly written?\n   - Are the 16 tables and multiple experiments well-organized?\n   - Does the paper read as a coherent narrative or a disconnected set of experiments?\n\n### Output Format\nProvide:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors**\n- **Missing Related Work or Conceptual Gaps**\n- **Score** (1-6) with justification\n- **Confidence** (1-5)","reviewer_id":"2","project":"gen-depth-fingerprint"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1322,"cache_read_input_tokens":60760,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1322},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb92a2fa-f385-427e-9d7a-ed913da4b34a","uuid":"61a89191-5740-4752-af9b-6f7f37497a8d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KyvCa3D6zFeJaDds97f4em","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_999770e4e57bfc6eabd12fc1f64af1ef:reviewer_2\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb92a2fa-f385-427e-9d7a-ed913da4b34a","uuid":"fe6a89fa-8573-44c7-b116-9818cc54a5ab","timestamp":"2026-05-20T10:56:59.648Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_999770e4e57bfc6eabd12fc1f64af1ef:reviewer_2\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group:oc_999770e4e57bfc6eabd12fc1f64af1ef:reviewer_2\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Bc9AUb3iB1a4Cq5u89smDk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewer tasks dispatched successfully. Now I need to actually conduct the reviews myself (as the Dream Review Director, I act as both reviewers since this is a simulated review). Let me proceed with the full roundtable protocol:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews (R1 and R2) — I'll write these now\nStep 2: Cross-share findings\nStep 3: Convergence discussion\nStep 4: Consolidated report + submit\n\nLet me write thorough independent reviews for both reviewers, then proceed through the roundtable.\n\n### R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\nLet me analyze the paper carefully from a methods/experiments perspective.\n\n\nThe LOGO binary F1 of 0.680 is concerning—it's only modestly above chance for a gen-1 vs gen-2+ classification task, and practical detectors typically need F1 >0.90. More troubling is that surface-only features achieve 95.9% of full performance with just a 3.2 percentage point gap (0.741 vs 0.773), and the marginal significance (p = 0.027) suggests reference features may not justify their computational overhead. The LOFO score of 0.665 trails LOGO by 1.5 points, indicating potential issues with feature importance estimation.\n\nThe 5-class results are particularly revealing—an F1 of 0.468 with heavy confusion between generations 2-4 suggests the underlying signal only supports roughly 2.5 effective classes rather than true ordinal depth. Even more damaging, a 4-detector ensemble with surface features (0.708) outperforms the proposed 7-feature approach (0.680) by 2.8 points, directly contradicting the method's contribution. QLoRA shows dramatic within-distribution dominance at 0.842 versus 0.694 in the binary case, though this advantage doesn't consistently transfer to the 3-class setting via LOGO. The practical applicability is further constrained by reliance on base models only, sensitivity to temperature settings, fragility to edits, domain dependence, and scale sensitivity—a combination that severely limits real-world deployment.\n\nThe downstream findings are uniformly negative: depth-aware curation performs no better than random subsampling, human edits degrade performance by 4.6-5.3 points (and AI text is routinely edited in practice), reference entropy drift varies twofold across models despite consistent direction, and length-matched evaluation drops F1 by 4.6 points, indicating length was driving part of the signal. The statistical significance itself is marginal—the 7-feature versus average log-probability comparison yields p = 0.038, but after Bonferroni correction across four comparisons this becomes p = 0.152, falling below significance.\n\nPrompt type creates a 5.1 point spread in performance, with continuation prompts performing best despite being most artificial. Cross-model chains show stronger binary F1 (0.764 vs 0.694) and LOGO scores (0.830 vs 0.694), though this might just be detecting generator switching rather than genuine depth understanding. The statistical picture remains shaky: McNemar's test gives p = 0.027 (marginal), bootstrap p = 0.038 (borderline and doesn't survive Bonferroni), and multiple comparison corrections haven't been applied consistently throughout.\n\nLooking at novelty and related work, the core issue is that DDR appears to be repackaging vocabulary impoverishment—all the drifting features like hapax legomena, type-token ratio, and entropy mean are just measuring lexical diversity declining with depth, which is already well-documented.\n\nThe task formulation itself seems like a modest extension: the 5-class F1 of 0.468 with confusion between generations 2-4 suggests the real signal is essentially binary with noise, so adding one class beyond binary detection doesn't meaningfully increase resolution. The contribution gets further undermined by the fact that a simple ensemble of existing detectors (0.708) outperforms the proposed method (0.680), which raises questions about whether purpose-built features actually add value. Even the downstream utility experiment—showing that depth-aware curation performs no better than random subsampling—is honest but problematic as a contribution.\n\nThere are also notable gaps in related work: watermarking literature on provenance, multi-class AI detection beyond binary classification, iterative self-refinement approaches, and broader stylometric analysis of machine-generated text. The drift characterization itself is partially confounded by reference model choice, with magnitude varying 2× across different models, so DDR isn't purely intrinsic but partly a function of which generator you're comparing against.\n\nThe paper tries to pack 15 experiments into 7 pages of main content, which creates a dense presentation that reads more like an experiment dump than a coherent narrative. There's also a framing mismatch—the paper positions itself as a classification tool, but the evidence really points toward it being an empirical study of recursive generation dynamics.\n\nNow I need to write out the full independent reviews, cross-share findings, and work toward convergence on a consolidated report. Let me think through what scores make sense given the significant weaknesses here—performance limitations, scope constraints, and the framing issue suggest this lands in the weaker tier. Now I'm assigning scores for the methods and novelty dimensions—the experimental approach is technically competent but the findings are underwhelming, while the novelty claim doesn't hold up against existing baselines, bringing the overall assessment to around 2.5 on average.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2860,"cache_read_input_tokens":62082,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2860},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb92a2fa-f385-427e-9d7a-ed913da4b34a","uuid":"a2c28ca5-a3cd-418e-b593-3a5760105d0c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Bc9AUb3iB1a4Cq5u89smDk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review tasks dispatched to both reviewers. Now I'll conduct the full 4-step roundtable process: independent reviews, cross-sharing, convergence, and consolidated report.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Independent Reviews**\n\n### R1 Independent Review — Methods & Experiments"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2860,"cache_read_input_tokens":62082,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2860},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb92a2fa-f385-427e-9d7a-ed913da4b34a","uuid":"38cef560-f96f-48fd-938c-e7f0925384db"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Bc9AUb3iB1a4Cq5u89smDk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Vm6Sx91N3pfbr4gK2nwSnj","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## R1 Independent Review Complete\n\n### Summary\nThis paper presents a 7-feature distributional ensemble for classifying generation depth (gen-0/1/2+) using DDR (drifting vs stationary features). While the experimental coverage is impressively comprehensive (15 conditions), the core results are underwhelming: LOGO binary F1 = 0.680 represents only moderate discriminability, and a trivially-constructed multi-detector ensemble outperforms the proposed method. Multiple compounding scope limitations severely narrow practical applicability.\n\n### Strengths\n- **S1**: Thorough evaluation methodology. LOGO and LOFO protocols are well-designed for measuring cross-generator generalization. The authors correctly identify and report within-family inflation (~3.8 pp for Pythia).\n- **S2**: The TOST equivalence test for the negative downstream finding is properly designed (±2% margin, p = 1.1×10⁻⁴, 3 random seeds, cross-seed std < 0.002). This is a model of how to report negative results.\n- **S3**: Comprehensive experimental coverage: 15 distinct experimental conditions covering ablation, generalization, robustness, baselines, and scale. Few papers test this many conditions.\n- **S4**: Honest reporting of scope limitations (instruct collapse, scale degradation, edit fragility, domain dependence). The paper does not overclaim.\n- **S5**: Length control analysis (§4.14) properly addresses a potential confound, showing depth signal survives length matching (−4.6 pp drop but trends preserved).\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1 [Major]**: LOGO binary F1 = 0.680 is insufficient for practical depth classification. For context, binary AI-text detectors achieve >0.95 F1. The 0.680 number means ~32% of gen-1/gen-2+ distinctions are wrong, which is too high for any downstream application (provenance auditing, contamination flagging, etc.).\n- **W2 [Major]**: The multi-detector ensemble (4-det + surface, F1 = 0.708) surpasses the proposed 7-feature ensemble (0.680) by 2.8 pp (p = 0.024). This directly undermines the contribution: if existing detectors can be trivially ensembled to outperform purpose-built depth features, the depth-specific feature engineering adds no value. The paper acknowledges this in Discussion but does not adequately address it.\n- **W3 [Major]**: Surface-only features capture 95.9% of full performance (0.741/0.773). The 3.2 pp gap (McNemar p = 0.027) is marginal and does not justify the computational cost of a 410M reference model forward pass. After Bonferroni correction for multiple ablation comparisons, p = 0.027 would likely not survive.\n- **W4 [Major]**: Compounding scope limitations. Each limitation individually is manageable, but they compound multiplicatively: base models only × moderate temperature × unedited text × Wikipedia-domain × continuation prompts. Under the most favorable conditions (Wikipedia + continuation + base model + unedited), F1 is 0.724. Under more realistic conditions (scientific domain + elaboration + some editing), performance likely drops to ~0.58–0.62, near chance for binary.\n- **W5 [Major]**: 5-class F1 = 0.468 with gen-2/3/4 near-uniform confusion (43–51% confusion rates) demonstrates the depth signal supports at most ~2.5 effective classes. Ordinal methods (CORAL, Frank-Hall) provide no improvement. This undermines the \"depth classification\" framing—the method cannot actually estimate depth, only roughly separate \"early\" from \"late.\"\n- **W6 [Moderate]**: QLoRA within-distribution F1 = 0.842 vs 0.694 (+14.8 pp) shows the proposed features miss substantial signal. While LOGO QLoRA 3-class drops below 7-feature (0.743 vs 0.762), LOGO binary QLoRA still wins (0.712 vs 0.694). The efficiency argument (1× vs 47× cost) is weakened by the magnitude of the gap.\n- **W7 [Moderate]**: Scale degradation (8B: −10.6 pp vs average, feature compression ratio 0.58) with a clear downward trend from 1B to 8B suggests extrapolation to frontier models (70B+) would yield near-chance performance—exactly the models generating the \"contamination crisis\" the paper claims to address.\n- **W8 [Minor]**: Bootstrap p = 0.038 for 7-feat vs Avg-LogProb is borderline significant. After Bonferroni correction for 4 baseline comparisons: adjusted p ≈ 0.152, not significant. The paper does not apply multiple comparison correction to any of its statistical tests.\n- **W9 [Minor]**: Cross-model chain improvement (+7.0 pp binary, +13.6 pp LOGO) may primarily detect generator switching rather than depth, since heterogeneous generators amplify distributional shift trivially.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. If Bonferroni correction is applied to the 4 baseline comparisons in Table 16, does the 7-feature advantage over Avg-LogProb remain significant?\n2. What is the expected F1 under realistic compounded conditions (e.g., scientific domain + elaboration prompt + 5% human editing + 8B generator)?\n3. Given that multi-detector ensemble outperforms, what is the practical scenario where the proposed features would be preferred?\n4. The 3.2 pp surface-vs-full gap: have you computed the cost-benefit of the reference model forward pass per pp of improvement?\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n- No instruct-model experiments (acknowledged as limitation but critical for practical relevance)\n- No temperature sensitivity analysis beyond the stated [0.5, 0.9] range\n- No compounded-condition analysis (domain × prompt × edit simultaneously)\n- Statistical power analysis for the LOFO evaluation (only 3 families → 3 folds)\n\n### Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\nThe experimental methodology is generally sound and the coverage is impressive, but the core result (F1 = 0.680) is too weak to support the depth classification framing, existing baselines outperform, and scope limitations compound to an impractically narrow operating range.\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\nHigh confidence in the methods and statistics assessment. Slight uncertainty on whether the community would value the empirical characterization independent of classification performance.","reviewer_id":"1","project":"gen-depth-fingerprint"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2860,"cache_read_input_tokens":62082,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2860},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb92a2fa-f385-427e-9d7a-ed913da4b34a","uuid":"19b815aa-08a2-4c69-9abe-6e73557057c7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vm6Sx91N3pfbr4gK2nwSnj","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_999770e4e57bfc6eabd12fc1f64af1ef:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb92a2fa-f385-427e-9d7a-ed913da4b34a","uuid":"b4c0d99c-94c5-47a7-8460-6396dc002d9f","timestamp":"2026-05-20T10:58:20.065Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_999770e4e57bfc6eabd12fc1f64af1ef:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group:oc_999770e4e57bfc6eabd12fc1f64af1ef:reviewer_1\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Bc9AUb3iB1a4Cq5u89smDk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017P4pALxjzvnD4h1PKYFysf","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## R2 Independent Review Complete\n\n### Summary\nThis paper proposes generation depth classification as a new task and introduces \"differential depth response\" (DDR) as the underlying phenomenon. While the task formulation is a reasonable extension of binary detection, the novelty is limited: DDR appears to be a repackaging of well-known vocabulary impoverishment, the 3-class formulation is a minimal extension of binary, and the proposed method is outperformed by a simple ensemble of existing detectors. The honest negative finding (depth curation ≈ random subsampling) further weakens the motivation.\n\n### Strengths\n- **S1**: The paper identifies a genuine gap: binary detection conflates qualitatively different generation depths. The task formulation is logically motivated even if the execution falls short.\n- **S2**: The drifting/stationary feature categorization, while potentially overstated, does provide a useful conceptual framework for understanding how distributional features respond to recursive generation. The direction-consistent, magnitude-varying pattern across reference models is an interesting empirical observation.\n- **S3**: The negative downstream finding is a genuine scientific contribution. Demonstrating that depth-aware curation provides no benefit over random subsampling (with proper TOST confirmation) is valuable for the community and saves future researchers from pursuing this direction.\n- **S4**: The paper is generally well-written and organized despite covering 15 experiments in compact space. The Discussion section honestly acknowledges major limitations.\n- **S5**: Cross-model chain evaluation (§4.8) and paraphrastic comparison (§4.7) provide novel experimental conditions that extend the model collapse literature.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1 [Major — Novelty]**: DDR is largely a repackaging of vocabulary impoverishment. All three \"drifting\" features (hapax ratio, TTR, entropy_mean) measure lexical diversity from different angles—they decline with depth because recursive generation progressively impoverishes vocabulary, a phenomenon extensively documented in model collapse literature (Shumailov et al. 2024, Briesch et al. 2023). Calling this \"differential depth response\" and contrasting it with \"stationary\" shape features provides a modest organizational insight, not a new phenomenon. The entropy_std R² = 0.41 sitting in the \"ambiguous\" zone further weakens the claimed clean dichotomy—this is a gradient, not a bimodal split.\n- **W2 [Major — Novelty]**: The 3-class task formulation is a minimal extension of binary detection. 5-class F1 = 0.468 with gen-2/3/4 forming a near-uniform confusion block demonstrates the signal supports approximately 2.5 effective classes. The method can roughly separate human/gen-1/gen-2+, but the gen-1 vs gen-2+ distinction is the only new capability beyond binary—and it achieves only 0.680 F1. This is one additional class at modest accuracy, not a fundamentally new task.\n- **W3 [Major — Contribution]**: The multi-detector ensemble (0.708) outperforming the proposed 7-feature ensemble (0.680) fundamentally undermines the contribution. If existing detectors can be trivially repurposed for depth estimation—and outperform purpose-built features—the paper's reason to exist is in question. The authors mention this in Discussion but offer no convincing counter-argument.\n- **W4 [Major — Motivation]**: The negative downstream finding, while scientifically honest, weaponizes the paper's own motivation. If depth has no utility for data curation (the most obvious application), and the \"diagnostic\" alternative framing offers only 0.680 F1, the practical impact is minimal. The paper needed to demonstrate at least one downstream task where depth matters.\n- **W5 [Moderate — Related Work]**: The paper insufficiently differentiates from the vocabulary impoverishment / model collapse literature. Shumailov et al. (2024) already demonstrate progressive distributional drift in iterative generation. Briesch et al. (2023) characterize information degradation across chain steps. The paper's contribution over these works is the ordinal classification formulation and cross-generator evaluation, but this incremental contribution is weakened by W1–W4.\n- **W6 [Moderate — Related Work]**: Missing related work: (a) Multi-class AI text detection approaches beyond binary; (b) Authorship attribution / stylometry literature on machine text (which has explored feature sets very similar to TTR, hapax, entropy); (c) Data provenance methods beyond detection (watermarking, membership inference); (d) Iterative self-refinement literature, where generation depth has a different but related meaning.\n- **W7 [Moderate — Framing]**: The paper is positioned as a \"classification tool\" but the evidence better supports an \"empirical characterization study.\" The tool framing invites practical criticism (performance, scope), while the characterization framing would be more defensible. The current framing creates a mismatch between promise and delivery.\n- **W8 [Minor]**: Reference-model dependence of DDR magnitude (2× variation, Pythia vs OLMo) suggests DDR partially captures reference-generator mismatch rather than intrinsic recursive generation properties. While direction is consistent, this complicates the \"intrinsic property\" framing.\n- **W9 [Minor]**: LD-Score addition (+0.6 pp) confirms that character-level signal is subsumed by word-level features, reinforcing the interpretation that all features measure the same underlying vocabulary impoverishment.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. How do you distinguish DDR from vocabulary impoverishment measured at different granularities? What specific aspect of DDR is not captured by the model collapse literature?\n2. If the multi-detector ensemble outperforms, what is the unique value proposition of depth-specific features?\n3. Can you identify any downstream task where knowing generation depth (beyond binary) provides actionable value?\n4. Given the 5-class collapse, would you agree that the task is more accurately described as \"2.5-class classification\" rather than \"depth classification\"?\n\n### Missing Related Work or Conceptual Gaps\n- Jovanović et al. (if applicable) on multi-class AI text source attribution\n- Watermarking literature (Kirchenbauer et al. 2023) as alternative provenance mechanism\n- Broader stylometry/authorship attribution literature using similar feature sets\n- Iterative self-refinement / chain-of-thought / recursive prompting as related forms of \"generation depth\"\n- The vocabulary impoverishment literature needs much stronger differentiation\n\n### Score: 2 (Reject)\nThe novelty is insufficient for a top venue. DDR repackages known vocabulary impoverishment. The task formulation is a minimal extension of binary detection. The proposed method is outperformed by a trivial baseline ensemble. The negative downstream finding, while honest, removes the primary motivation. The paper would benefit from repositioning as an empirical characterization study and identifying at least one scenario where depth knowledge provides actionable value.\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\nConfident in the novelty assessment and related work evaluation. Some uncertainty about whether the community places higher value on comprehensive empirical characterization than I'm currently crediting.","reviewer_id":"2","project":"gen-depth-fingerprint"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2860,"cache_read_input_tokens":62082,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2860},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb92a2fa-f385-427e-9d7a-ed913da4b34a","uuid":"af45bff8-e2ee-4de6-85b8-f2d8f659ce72"}
